WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
VOICEOVER: Meet ART. The Amphibious Robot Turtle.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:10.740
As its name suggests art is a robot&nbsp;
that can swim in water and walk on land.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:17.820
And it's all down to a clever party trick.&nbsp;
It has legs that can turn into flippers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.140
According to the team of researchers&nbsp;
behind the 'bot', this shape-shifting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.225
ability has the potential to make art more&nbsp;
efficient than existing amphibious machines.

00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:30.240
ROBERT BAINES: So some robot&nbsp;
designers will say, okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:36.060
we know propellers work well in water&nbsp;
to move around, we know wheels work well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.800
on land - so let's make a robot - put&nbsp;
propellers on it and put wheels on it.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.880
In water a propeller might be&nbsp;
very efficient but on land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.900
it doesn't really allow the robot to move.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.280
But it ends up being additional&nbsp;
weight on the robot, additional space.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.120
VOICEOVER: This is where art&nbsp;
makes its efficiency gains.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.920
Instead of having separate components&nbsp;
to move in water and on land, ART's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.420
morphing limbs can adapt to suit both,&nbsp;
removing the need for redundant parts.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:12.480
ROBERT BAINES: So these are the two&nbsp;
modes of the morphing limb. So on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:18.480
my right here we have the flipper mode,&nbsp;
which is this flat hydrodynamic shape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:25.920
And then on my left here we have the leg&nbsp;
mode which is this shape - cylindrical and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.420
load bearing and we have a little curvature&nbsp;
on the end of the limb that gives it some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.680
surface area so that it can contact&nbsp;
the ground and not not just fall over.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.460
VOICEOVER: Creating this limb&nbsp;
required some principles from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.085
the rapidly growing field of soft robotics.

00:01:41.085 --> 00:01:44.280
ROBERT BAINES: on the outside there's&nbsp;
a balloon that inflates and gives the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:50.160
limb its shape and on the inside, bonded to&nbsp;
this balloon, is a material that changes its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:55.800
stiffness. When it's heated up past a certain&nbsp;
temperature it becomes very soft and pliable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:01.920
And so using these materials we can transition&nbsp;
from The flipper to the leg or from the leg to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.760
the flipper, that can walk on land or swim&nbsp;
in water - or transition between the two.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.200
VOICEOVER: But creating adaptable&nbsp;
limbs was just the first step.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.600
Next they needed to program ART with&nbsp;
patterns of movement known as gaits to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.160
make use of these morphing materials. And&nbsp;
for inspiration as you might have guessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.240
they turned to turtles and their&nbsp;
terrestrial counterparts tortoises.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.640
ROBERT BAINES: So the walking gait is&nbsp;
called a creeping gait. This isn't the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.480
fastest gait but it's similar&nbsp;
to a tortoise - so if you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.220
at a tortoise they have sort of this amble&nbsp;
where they're lifting their legs up slowly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.840
one by one. And this is what this&nbsp;
creeping gait was inspired by.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:49.680
So we also have a gate that's inspired more&nbsp;
by, for example sea turtles, and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.140
the quintessential flapping motion you&nbsp;
would see a sea turtle doing - sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.580
a quasi-figure eight pattern in which the&nbsp;
flipper is coming up and then coming down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.040
and it's changing its angle with respect&nbsp;
to the the forward direction all the while.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:11.580
VOICEOVER: These two gates seem to do the job&nbsp;
well on land and in water but ART ran into some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:18.720
difficulty on the awkward terrain between the two&nbsp;
environments which is often sandy stony or muddy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:25.185
and ART often slipped or got stuck. So the&nbsp;
team opted for another less elegant solution.

00:03:25.185 --> 00:03:30.600
ROBERT BAINES: So we looked at beaching&nbsp;
sea turtles and basically the locomotion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.400
strategy they use, which is lying&nbsp;
on their stomachs and then pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.800
themselves forward. And is not particularly&nbsp;
graceful but shifting the gait really gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.400
us a little more versatility&nbsp;
in where the robot could go.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:50.280
VOICEOVER: The researchers calculated metrics for&nbsp;
ART's efficiency by measuring how much power it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:55.380
used and how fast it was able to move for its&nbsp;
weight in different settings. And armed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:01.200
its morphing limbs and shifting gaits, ART was&nbsp;
able to match or outperform many exclusively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:07.200
terrestrial or exclusively aquatic robots - while&nbsp;
still being able to move in both environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:13.860
Of course art is still a work in progress. Right&nbsp;
now, for example, it's tethered meaning its power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.600
supply and programming centre aren't housed&nbsp;
within the body itself. But in the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.580
the team hopes that morphing amphibious&nbsp;
'bots' loaded with sensors or cameras like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:29.400
you can see here could be used for things like&nbsp;
ecological monitoring or even disaster response.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.305
And their shape-shifting approach to&nbsp;
robot design doesn't have to stop there.

00:04:34.305 --> 00:04:39.360
ROBERT BAINES: So I don't think it's necessarily&nbsp;
confined to just a robot with morphing limbs.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:46.980
This idea can be extended where we have robot&nbsp;
bodies that morph more drastically to specialise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:52.920
for different types of locomotion, different&nbsp;
tasks. I think if people see the potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:59.340
efficiency gains, shape morphing lends itself to&nbsp;
adaptability in a variety of robotic applications

